<description>Simple exponential smoothing</description>
<tags>C53</tags>
<label>SES</label>
<menu-attachment>MAINWIN/Variable/Filter</menu-attachment>
<help filename="ses_help.md">
# ses

Simple exponential smoothing (ETS(A,N,N)) for gretl, following
[fpp3, section 8.1](https://otexts.com/fpp3/ses.html).

The level evolves as

    l_t = alpha * y_t + (1 - alpha) * l_{t-1}

The one-step forecast of `y_t` is `l_{t-1}`, and all multi-step forecasts are
flat at the last level. The smoothing parameter `alpha` (restricted to [0,1])
and the initial level `l_0` are estimated jointly by minimizing the sum of
squared one-step forecast errors (BFGS with box constraints on `alpha`).

Requires time-series data and gretl 2024c or later.

## Public functions

### `bundle ses(const series y, bundle specs[null])`

Estimates the model and returns a bundle. Optional keys of `specs`:

| key       | description                                                          | default |
|-----------|----------------------------------------------------------------------|---------|
| `alpha`   | Fixed smoothing parameter in [0,1]; if absent, it is estimated       | -       |
| `init`    | Fixed initial level `l_0`; if absent, it is estimated                | -       |
| `ntest`   | Number of trailing observations held back to test the forecasts      | 0       |
| `verbose` | Verbosity, 0 to 3                                                    | 1       |

The training range is the contiguous non-missing range of `y`, minus the last
`ntest` observations. If `ntest` is 0 and the sample contains observations
beyond the end of `y` (e.g. added by `dataset addobs`), flat out-of-sample
forecasts are computed for them. Missing values inside `y` are an error.

Returned bundle:

| key          | description                                                         |
|--------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------|
| `alpha`      | Smoothing parameter                                                 |
| `l0`         | Initial level                                                       |
| `theta`      | Vector (alpha, l0)                                                  |
| `SSE`        | Training sum of squared one-step errors                             |
| `RMSE_train` | Training root mean squared error                                    |
| `MAE_train`  | Training mean absolute error                                        |
| `RMSE_test`, `MAE_test` | Test statistics, only if `ntest &gt; 0`                     |
| `H`          | Matrix with columns `y`, `level`, `forecast` and, if there are forecasts beyond the training range, `stderr` |
| `ntest`      | Number of test observations                                         |
| `yname`      | Name of the series                                                  |
| `limits`     | Vector (start of training, end of training, end of forecasts)       |

In `H`, `forecast` is the one-step-ahead forecast inside the training range and
the flat multi-step forecast beyond it. `stderr` follows fpp3 (section 8.7):
`sigma * sqrt(1 + (h-1) * alpha^2)`, with `sigma^2 = RMSE_train^2`.

With gretl 2026b or later the bundle also holds the virtual series
`&lt;y&gt;_level`, `&lt;y&gt;_fcast` and `&lt;y&gt;_irreg`.

### `void ses_plot(const bundleref b, int ptype, matrix ci_levels[null])`

`ptype = 1` (default): actual and forecast, with confidence bands if forecasts
beyond the training range exist. `ptype = 2`: level and irregular component.
`ci_levels` holds two confidence levels between 50 and 99 (default `{80, 95}`).

### `matrix ses_ci_matrix(const bundle b, matrix ci_levels[null])`

Returns a matrix with the lower and upper bounds (`Lo80`, `Hi80`, `Lo95`,
`Hi95`) of the forecast intervals; requires forecasts beyond the training range.

### `bundle ses_gui(...)`

Graphical interface, available under *Variable &gt; Filter &gt; SES*. With `parms`
set to `automatic`, `alpha` is estimated; with `pre-specified` the given
`alpha` is used. The initial level is always estimated. `ntest` and `verbose`
as above.

## Example

    include ses.gfn
    open algeria.gdt --frompkg=ses
    dataset addobs 5
    bundle b = ses(Exports)
    print ses_ci_matrix(b)
    ses_plot(&amp;b, 1)

## Reference

Hyndman, R.J., and Athanasopoulos, G. (2021) *Forecasting: Principles and
Practice*, 3rd edition, OTexts. &lt;https://otexts.com/fpp3/&gt;
</help>
<data-files count="1">
algeria.gdt </data-files>
<gretl-function name="ses" type="bundle">
 <params count="2">
  <param name="y" type="series" const="true"/>
  <param name="specs" type="bundle" optional="true" const="true"/>
 </params>
<code>/* Simple exponential smoothing of series 'y'.

specs.alpha:   smoothing parameter in [0,1]. If given, alpha is not
estimated.
specs.init:    initial level l_0. If given, l_0 is not estimated.
specs.ntest:   number of trailing observations held back for testing
forecasts (default 0). If 0 and the sample holds
trailing observations beyond the range of y, they are
used as the out-of-sample forecast range.
specs.verbose: 0 to 3 (default 1) */

string yname = argname(y, &quot;y&quot;)

bundle args = ses_default_args()
scalar fixed_alpha = 0
scalar fixed_init = 0
if exists(specs)
  fixed_alpha = inbundle(specs, &quot;alpha&quot;)
  fixed_init = inbundle(specs, &quot;init&quot;)
  errorif(bcheck(&amp;args, specs), &quot;aborting&quot;)
endif

scalar ntest = args.ntest
scalar verbose = args.verbose

matrix limits = ses_ranges(y, ntest)
scalar t1 = limits[1]
scalar t2 = limits[2]
scalar t3 = limits[3]
smpl t1 t3

series a f
list L = y a f

if verbose &gt; 0
  printf &quot;Simple exponential smoothing\n&quot;
  printf &quot;Training range: %s to %s\n&quot;, obslabel(t1), obslabel(t2)
  if args.ntest &gt; 0
    printf &quot;Testing forecast: %s to %s\n\n&quot;, obslabel(t2+1), obslabel(t3)
  elif t3 &gt; t2
    printf &quot;Out-of-sample forecast: %s to %s\n\n&quot;, obslabel(t2+1), obslabel(t3)
  else
    printf &quot;Number of forecasts: 0\n\n&quot;
  endif
endif

# training data as a column vector
smpl t1 t2
matrix my = {y}
smpl t1 t3

matrix theta = {args.alpha; my[1]}
if fixed_init
  theta[2] = args.init
endif

if !(fixed_alpha &amp;&amp; fixed_init)
  theta = ses_auto_parms(my, theta, !fixed_alpha, !fixed_init, verbose)
  if !ok(theta[1])
    funcerr &quot;Failed to estimate parameters&quot;
  endif
endif

scalar alpha = theta[1]
scalar l0 = theta[2]

# training: levels and one-step-ahead forecasts
matrix Ltr = ses_levels(theta, my)
matrix u = my - Ltr[,1]
scalar n = rows(u)
scalar SSE = sumc(u.^2)
scalar RMSE_train = sqrt(SSE / n)
scalar MAE_train = sumc(abs(u)) / n

# transcribe to series over t1..t2
smpl t1 t2
a = Ltr[,2]
f = Ltr[,1]

# flat forecasts over t2+1..t3 (test or out-of-sample)
if t3 &gt; t2
  smpl t2+1 t3
  f = Ltr[n,2]
endif
smpl t1 t3

# same names whether or not we are printing, so that ses_plot()
# and other consumers of H always find them
rename a level
rename f forecast

if verbose &gt; 0
  printf &quot;alpha = %g, l_0 = %g\n&quot;, alpha, l0
  printf &quot;training SSE = %g, RMSE = %g, MAE = %g\n&quot;, SSE, RMSE_train, MAE_train
  print L -o
endif

set skip_missing off
matrix H = {L}
bundle ret = _(alpha, l0, theta, ntest, H, SSE, RMSE_train, MAE_train, yname, limits)

if ntest &gt; 0
  ses_add_eval_stats(&amp;ret)
  if verbose &gt; 0
    printf &quot;testing RMSE = %g, MAE = %g\n&quot;, ret.RMSE_test, ret.MAE_test
  endif
endif

if verbose &gt; 0
  printf &quot;\n&quot;
endif
if ret.limits[3] &gt; ret.limits[2]
  ses_add_fcse(&amp;ret)
endif
if $version &gt;= 20262
  ses_add_virtual_series(&amp;ret)
endif

return ret
</code>
</gretl-function>
<gretl-function name="ses_gui" type="bundle" pkg-role="gui-main">
 <params count="5">
  <param name="y" type="series"/>
  <param name="parms" type="int" min="1" max="2" default="1">
<description>alpha</description>
<labels count="2">
"automatic" "pre-specified" </labels>
  </param>
  <param name="alpha" type="scalar" min="0" max="1" default="0.2" step="0.0001">
<description>smoothing parameter, α</description>
  </param>
  <param name="ntest" type="int" min="0" default="0">
<description>ntest, see Help</description>
  </param>
  <param name="verbose" type="int" min="0" max="3" default="1"/>
 </params>
<code>/* GUI wrapper around ses(). */

string yname = argname(y, &quot;y&quot;)
if yname != &quot;y&quot;
  rename y @yname
endif

bundle ret
if parms == 1
  ret = ses(@yname, _(ntest, verbose))
else
  ret = ses(@yname, _(alpha, ntest, verbose))
endif
return ret
</code>
</gretl-function>
<gretl-function name="ses_plot" type="void" pkg-role="bundle-plot">
 <params count="3">
  <param name="b" type="bundleref" const="true"/>
  <param name="ptype" type="int" min="1" max="2" default="1">
<description>plot type</description>
<labels count="2">
"actual and forecast" "components" </labels>
  </param>
  <param name="ci_levels" type="matrix" optional="true"/>
 </params>
<code>/* Plot the results of ses(): either actual, fitted and forecast
(with confidence bands, if forecasts are available), or the level
and the irregular component. */

list L = mat2list(b.H)
if ptype == 1
  string yname = b.yname
  if yname != &quot;y&quot;
    rename y @yname
  endif
  if b.limits[3] &gt; b.limits[2]
    scalar c1 c2 hmax
    ses_get_ci_vals(b, ci_levels, &amp;c1, &amp;c2, &amp;hmax)
    df = rows(b.H) - hmax - 2
    hbands = ses_ci_bands(df, c1, c2)
    list P = @yname forecast
    plot P
      options --time-series --with-lines --bands=hbands
      printf &quot;set title 'forecast with %d and %d percent intervals'&quot;, c1, c2
    end plot --output=display
  else
    gnuplot @yname forecast --time --with-lines --output=display
  endif
else
  series irreg = b.H[,1] - b.H[,3]
  tsplots level irreg --output=display
endif
</code>
</gretl-function>
<gretl-function name="ses_ci_matrix" type="matrix">
 <params count="2">
  <param name="b" type="bundle" const="true"/>
  <param name="ci_levels" type="matrix" optional="true"/>
 </params>
<code>/* Confidence intervals for the post-training forecasts: columns
Lo/Hi for the lower and upper of the two requested levels
(default 80 and 95 percent). */

hmax = b.limits[3] - b.limits[2]
errorif(hmax &lt;= 0, &quot;No post-training forecasts are available&quot;)
if exists(ci_levels)
  ses_check_ci_levels(ci_levels)
else
  ci_levels = {80, 95}
endif

df = rows(b.H) - hmax - 2
ci1 = min(ci_levels)
ci2 = max(ci_levels)
f1 = critical(t, df, (100 - ci1) / 200)
f2 = critical(t, df, (100 - ci2) / 200)

matrix CI = zeros(hmax, 4)
t = rows(b.H) - hmax + 1
scalar fc me
loop i=1..hmax
  fc = b.H[t,3]
  me = f1 * b.H[t,4]
  CI[i,1] = fc - me
  CI[i,2] = fc + me
  me = f2 * b.H[t,4]
  CI[i,3] = fc - me
  CI[i,4] = fc + me
  t++
endloop

strings cs = array(4)
cs[1] = sprintf(&quot;Lo%d&quot;, ci1)
cs[2] = sprintf(&quot;Hi%d&quot;, ci1)
cs[3] = sprintf(&quot;Lo%d&quot;, ci2)
cs[4] = sprintf(&quot;Hi%d&quot;, ci2)
cnameset(CI, cs)
return CI
</code>
</gretl-function>
<gretl-function name="ses_levels" type="matrix" private="1">
 <params count="2">
  <param name="theta" type="matrix" const="true"/>
  <param name="y" type="matrix" const="true"/>
 </params>
<code>/* SES recursion given theta = (alpha, l_0). Column 1: one-step
forecast made at t-1 (for t = 1 the initial level l_0); column 2:
level l_t = alpha*y_t + (1-alpha)*l_{t-1}. */

scalar a = theta[1]
scalar l = theta[2]
matrix L = zeros(rows(y), 2)
loop t=1..rows(y) -q
  L[t,1] = l
  l = a*y[t] + (1-a)*l
  L[t,2] = l
endloop
return L
</code>
</gretl-function>
<gretl-function name="ses_crit" type="scalar" private="1">
 <params count="4">
  <param name="p" type="matrix" const="true"/>
  <param name="my" type="matrix" const="true"/>
  <param name="theta0" type="matrix" const="true"/>
  <param name="idx" type="matrix" const="true"/>
 </params>
<code>/* Sum of squared one-step forecast errors as a function of the free
parameters 'p', which occupy positions 'idx' of theta = (alpha, l_0); the remaining elements are taken from 'theta0'. */

matrix theta = theta0
theta[idx] = p
matrix L = ses_levels(theta, my)
return sumc((my - L[,1]).^2)
</code>
</gretl-function>
<gretl-function name="ses_auto_parms" type="matrix" private="1">
 <params count="5">
  <param name="my" type="matrix" const="true"/>
  <param name="theta0" type="matrix" const="true"/>
  <param name="free_alpha" type="int"/>
  <param name="free_init" type="int"/>
  <param name="verbose" type="int"/>
 </params>
<code>/* Estimate the free elements of theta = (alpha, l_0) by minimising
the SSE. alpha is restricted to [0,1]; l_0 is unrestricted. Returns
the full theta, or theta[1] = NA on failure. */

matrix theta = theta0
matrix idx = selifr({1; 2}, {free_alpha; free_init})
matrix p = theta[idx]

if verbose == 3
  set max_verbose full
elif verbose == 2
  set max_verbose on
  set bfgs_verbskip 5
endif

scalar SSE
if free_alpha
  matrix bounds = {1, 0, 1}
  catch SSE = BFGScmin(&amp;p, bounds, ses_crit(p, my, theta0, idx))
else
  catch SSE = BFGSmin(&amp;p, ses_crit(p, my, theta0, idx))
endif
err = $error

if err
  printf &quot;Got error %d from the optimizer\n&quot;, err
  theta[1] = NA
else
  theta[idx] = p
  if verbose &gt; 1
    printf &quot;\n&quot;
  endif
endif
return theta
</code>
</gretl-function>
<gretl-function name="ses_ranges" type="matrix" private="1">
 <params count="2">
  <param name="y" type="series" const="true"/>
  <param name="ntest" type="int"/>
 </params>
<code>/* Returns (t1, t2, t3): start and end of the training range and end
of the forecast range. */

matrix ret = zeros(1,3)
catch smpl --contiguous y
if $error
  funcerr &quot;Missing values encountered&quot;
endif
ret[1] = $t1                # start of contiguous y
ret[2] = $t2                # end of contiguous y
scalar tail = $tmax - $t2
if ntest &gt; 0
  ret[2] = $t2 - ntest
  ret[3] = $t2
elif tail &gt; 0
  ret[3] = $tmax
else
  ret[3] = $t2
endif
ntrain = ret[2] - ret[1] + 1
errorif(ntrain &lt; 4, &quot;Insufficient observations for training&quot;)
return ret
</code>
</gretl-function>
<gretl-function name="ses_default_args" type="bundle" private="1">
<code>/* Default options and their admissible ranges for bcheck(). */

bundle b
b.alpha = 0.5               # start value if alpha is estimated
b.init = NA
b.ntest = 0
b.verbose = 1
b.bounds = _(alpha={0,1}, verbose={0,3}, ntest={0,NA})
return b
</code>
</gretl-function>
<gretl-function name="ses_add_eval_stats" type="void" private="1">
 <params count="1">
  <param name="b" type="bundleref"/>
 </params>
<code>/* Add RMSE and MAE over the test range to 'b'. */

scalar T = rows(b.H)
scalar t0 = T - b.ntest + 1
matrix e = b.H[t0:T,1] - b.H[t0:T,3]
b.RMSE_test = sqrt(sumc(e.^2) / b.ntest)
b.MAE_test = sumc(abs(e)) / b.ntest
</code>
</gretl-function>
<gretl-function name="ses_add_fcse" type="void" private="1">
 <params count="1">
  <param name="b" type="bundleref"/>
 </params>
<code>/* Append the forecast standard errors of ETS(A,N,N) as a column of
'b.H', see https://otexts.com/fpp3/ets-forecasting.html (sec. 8.7):
var_h = sigma^2 * (1 + (h-1)*alpha^2). */

scalar s2 = b.RMSE_train^2
scalar hmax = b.limits[3] - b.limits[2]
matrix vh = ones(rows(b.H), 1) * NA
scalar t = rows(vh) - hmax + 1
loop h=1..hmax -q
  vh[t++] = s2 * (1 + (h-1) * b.alpha^2)
endloop
strings ss = cnameget(b.H)
b.H ~= sqrt(vh)
cnameset(b.H, ss + &quot;stderr&quot;)
</code>
</gretl-function>
<gretl-function name="ses_add_virtual_series" type="void" private="1">
 <params count="1">
  <param name="b" type="bundleref"/>
 </params>
<code>/* Make level, forecast and irregular available as virtual series. */

string s = sprintf(&quot;%s_level&quot;, b.yname)
b[s] = &quot;virtual_series:H[,2]&quot;
s = sprintf(&quot;%s_fcast&quot;, b.yname)
b[s] = &quot;virtual_series:H[,3]&quot;
s = sprintf(&quot;%s_irreg&quot;, b.yname)
b[s] = &quot;virtual_series:H[,1]-H[,3]&quot;
</code>
</gretl-function>
<gretl-function name="ses_check_ci_levels" type="void" private="1">
 <params count="1">
  <param name="pc" type="matrix" const="true"/>
 </params>
<code>scalar err = 0
if nelem(pc) != 2
  err = 1
elif min(pc) &lt; 50 || max(pc) &gt; 99
  err = 2
elif pc[1] == pc[2]
  err = 3
endif
errorif(err, &quot;ci_levels: invalid argument&quot;)
</code>
</gretl-function>
<gretl-function name="ses_ci_bands" type="bundles" private="1">
 <params count="3">
  <param name="df" type="int"/>
  <param name="c1" type="scalar"/>
  <param name="c2" type="scalar"/>
 </params>
<code>bundles bb = array(2)
# for plotting, show the wider band first
f1 = critical(t, df, (100 - c2) / 200)
f2 = critical(t, df, (100 - c1) / 200)
bb[1] = _(center=&quot;forecast&quot;, width=&quot;stderr&quot;, factor=f1, style=&quot;fill&quot;, color=&quot;#BBD9F5&quot;)
bb[2] = _(center=&quot;forecast&quot;, width=&quot;stderr&quot;, factor=f2, style=&quot;fill&quot;, color=&quot;#6AADEC&quot;)
return bb
</code>
</gretl-function>
<gretl-function name="ses_get_ci_vals" type="void" private="1">
 <params count="5">
  <param name="b" type="bundle" const="true"/>
  <param name="ci_levels" type="matrix" optional="true"/>
  <param name="c1" type="scalarref"/>
  <param name="c2" type="scalarref"/>
  <param name="hmax" type="scalarref"/>
 </params>
<code>if exists(ci_levels)
  ses_check_ci_levels(ci_levels)
else
  ci_levels = {80, 95}
endif
hmax = b.limits[3] - b.limits[2]
c1 = min(ci_levels)
c2 = max(ci_levels)
</code>
</gretl-function>
<gretl-function name="ses_precheck" type="scalar" private="1" pkg-role="gui-precheck">
<code>/* Non-zero if the dataset is not suitable: we require sufficient
observations. */
return $nobs &lt; 10
</code>
</gretl-function>
<gretl-function name="ses_ui_maker" type="bundle" private="1" pkg-role="ui-maker">
<code>bundle b
b.alpha = _(depends=&quot;parms=2&quot;)
return b
</code>
</gretl-function>
<sample-script>
clear
set verbose off
include ses.gfn

# Annual exports of Algeria, 1960-2017
open algeria.gdt --frompkg=ses --quiet

# Estimate alpha and the initial level (fpp3: alpha = 0.84, l_0 = 39.5)
bundle b = ses(Exports)

# Fix alpha, hold back the last 5 observations for evaluation
bundle opts = _(alpha=0.5, ntest=5)
bundle b2 = ses(Exports, opts)

# Five out-of-sample forecasts with intervals and plots
dataset addobs 5
bundle b3 = ses(Exports)
print ses_ci_matrix(b3)
ses_plot(&amp;b3, 1)
ses_plot(&amp;b3, 2)
</sample-script>
</gretl-function-package>
</gretl-functions>
